# Part I — Attack the Branching-Compensation Claim

The proposed claim is roughly:

> In a minimal failed q-round burning strategy, a persistent obstruction can be arranged so that its relevant boundary does not contain branching vertices.

Do NOT assume this.

Try to prove or disprove the following precise statement:

### Claim A

Let T be a tree and suppose a chosen q-round burning strategy maximizes the eventual number of covered vertices in a suitable lexicographic sense.

If the final unburned component C has a boundary vertex v with

$$
\deg_T(v)\ge3,
$$

then there exists a modification of the burning sequence that is no worse and moves the obstruction away from v.

First determine whether Claim A is even true.

